WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:03.949
This is no ordinary rocket engine.

00:00:03.949 --> 00:00:10.050
Limited by the technology of their times engineers
and physicists have theorized this propulsion

00:00:10.050 --> 00:00:16.150
method for centuries, but the constraints
of time may have just lifted with NASA’s

00:00:16.150 --> 00:00:22.400
revolutionary test of this rotating detonation
engine in January 2023.

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:28.260
With the very latest advances in material
science and computational fluid dynamics,

00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:34.040
NASA has managed to break through to test
a 3D printed rocket engine that could improve

00:00:34.040 --> 00:00:37.640
fuel efficiencies of rockets by up to 5%.

00:00:37.640 --> 00:00:38.640
[1]

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:43.890
A 5% improvement in fuel efficiency for a
rocket engine is not a marginal gain.

00:00:43.890 --> 00:00:46.140
Rocket weights are dominated by fuel.

00:00:46.140 --> 00:00:53.129
The 1st stage Saturn V had a dry mass of 137,000
kilograms, when fully loaded with propellant

00:00:53.129 --> 00:00:56.570
its weight was 2.2 million kilograms.

00:00:56.570 --> 00:00:59.150
The first stage was 94% fuel.

00:00:59.150 --> 00:01:05.680
A 5% increase in fuel efficiency represents
a 103 tonne decrease in fuel.

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:09.920
That’s not far off the total dry weight
of the Falcon 9 rocket.

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:10.920
[2]

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:12.990
This does not just provide cost savings.

00:01:12.990 --> 00:01:17.180
It represents a drastic change in capabilities
of rockets.

00:01:17.180 --> 00:01:21.760
Allowing humans to launch larger payloads
further into the solar system.

00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:27.180
This latest test of the technology saw the
engine run 18 times, with a maximum firing

00:01:27.180 --> 00:01:30.390
duration of 113s seconds per run.

00:01:30.390 --> 00:01:31.390
[3]

00:01:31.390 --> 00:01:36.549
This was a test, and the engine is not ready
to use, but this test represents the culmination

00:01:36.549 --> 00:01:42.490
of decades of research, enabled by newly developed
metals and manufacturing techniques and the

00:01:42.490 --> 00:01:46.439
very latest advances in computational fluid
dynamic modeling.

00:01:46.439 --> 00:01:51.439
Let's delve into the science and engineering
behind Rotating detonation engines to see

00:01:51.439 --> 00:02:03.200
how they work.

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:06.729
Engines powered by detonations are not a new
concept.

00:02:06.729 --> 00:02:11.940
The Air Force Research Laboratory developed
this heavily modified Rutan Long EZ.

00:02:11.940 --> 00:02:16.530
Powered by 4 tubes in which the detonations
occurred.

00:02:16.530 --> 00:02:22.030
Traditional rocket engines work by mixing
fuel and an oxidizer, using a continuous igniter.

00:02:22.030 --> 00:02:26.450
With the ignitor typically located at the
top of the combustion chamber, a flame front

00:02:26.450 --> 00:02:30.230
needs to travel through the fuel mixture to
complete combustion.

00:02:30.230 --> 00:02:35.080
If the speed of this flame front is below
supersonic speed, the combustion is called

00:02:35.080 --> 00:02:36.280
deflagration.

00:02:36.280 --> 00:02:40.790
If the “flame front” travels through the
mixture at over Mach 1, then the process is

00:02:40.790 --> 00:02:42.909
called detonation.

00:02:42.909 --> 00:02:49.459
Almost all combustion engines work with deflagrations,
but as their name implies Rotating detonation

00:02:49.459 --> 00:02:56.190
engines use a supersonic flame front to produce
thrust, and as a result can produce more thrust

00:02:56.190 --> 00:02:58.239
for the same volume of fuel.

00:02:58.239 --> 00:03:01.409
However, this is easier said than done.

00:03:01.409 --> 00:03:07.220
The energy needed to initiate a detonation
is very large, and for these pulsed detonation

00:03:07.220 --> 00:03:12.030
engines used on the Air Forces test vehicle,
the detonations needed to happen hundreds

00:03:12.030 --> 00:03:13.709
of times a second.

00:03:13.709 --> 00:03:19.080
We can initiate a normal deflagration combustion
flame front, which travels along the tube

00:03:19.080 --> 00:03:24.670
and with some encouragement will transition
to a detonation, but the causes of this aren’t

00:03:24.670 --> 00:03:28.140
totally understood and are not always reliably
repeatable.

00:03:28.140 --> 00:03:32.790
We know that detonations happen more often
when the fuel is turbulent so to create these

00:03:32.790 --> 00:03:37.950
conditions, pulsed detonation engines often
include physical barriers.

00:03:37.950 --> 00:03:41.849
One method to achieve this is by placing a
spiral inside the tube.

00:03:41.849 --> 00:03:42.849
[4]

00:03:42.849 --> 00:03:44.930
Pulsed detonation has a more obvious problem.

00:03:44.930 --> 00:03:47.310
They don’t provide constant thrust.

00:03:47.310 --> 00:03:48.840
They pulse.

00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:52.860
Rotating detonation engines are the solution
to both of these problems.

00:03:52.860 --> 00:03:55.890
They consist of a ring shaped combustion chamber.

00:03:55.890 --> 00:04:01.080
The process starts by opening a valve at one
end of a tube and letting a fuel mixture fill

00:04:01.080 --> 00:04:02.209
the tube.

00:04:02.209 --> 00:04:07.110
The valve closes and an ignition source at
the closed section goes off and the detonation

00:04:07.110 --> 00:04:08.110
begins.

00:04:08.110 --> 00:04:13.810
A shockwave begins to travel around the circumference
of the ring that increases the pressure and

00:04:13.810 --> 00:04:17.579
temperature of the gas behind it almost instantaneously.

00:04:17.579 --> 00:04:23.180
This gas then expands and leaves the tube
at extremely high velocity, creating thrust.

00:04:23.180 --> 00:04:28.190
This cycle then repeats with the shockwave
traveling in a never ending circle.

00:04:28.190 --> 00:04:33.090
This clever arrangement solves the major problems
with pulsed detonation engines by having to

00:04:33.090 --> 00:04:38.800
only set off the detonation once, it eliminates
the unreliability of setting off thousands

00:04:38.800 --> 00:04:44.020
of detonations a second and its continual
spiral around the combustion chamber results

00:04:44.020 --> 00:04:45.490
in consistent thrust.

00:04:45.490 --> 00:04:52.300
Keep in mind these detonation fronts are going
FAST, operating at Mach 3-6 depending on the

00:04:52.300 --> 00:04:53.639
fuel mixture.

00:04:53.639 --> 00:04:58.850
This means that the waves complete a full
circle in a thousandth of a second, with operating

00:04:58.850 --> 00:05:02.009
ranges between 1 to 10 kHz [5].

00:05:02.009 --> 00:05:06.050
This is an incredibly difficult phenomenon
to harness.

00:05:06.050 --> 00:05:11.150
Detonations release energy very quickly, are
hard to control, and are unstable by nature.

00:05:11.150 --> 00:05:16.139
In fact, for the last century of aerospace
advancement, engineers have been working very

00:05:16.139 --> 00:05:20.590
hard to prevent detonations in their rocket
nozzles.

00:05:20.590 --> 00:05:25.040
During the development of the F1 engine in
the Apollo program, the engineers struggled

00:05:25.040 --> 00:05:29.230
to control what they called “thermodynamic
instabilities”.

00:05:29.230 --> 00:05:33.460
These were in fact detonations that were destroying
the engine.[6]

00:05:33.460 --> 00:05:40.270
On June 28th 1962 these combustion instabilities
caused an engine to be completely destroyed

00:05:40.270 --> 00:05:41.630
during testing.

00:05:41.630 --> 00:05:46.880
This problem was plaguing the engineers of
the Saturn V and is arguably the greatest

00:05:46.880 --> 00:05:51.790
challenge they faced in scaling this monstrous
rocket to reach the moon.

00:05:51.790 --> 00:05:56.230
A list of 18 potential sources of the instability
were made.

00:05:56.230 --> 00:05:59.790
This was an incredibly complex and dynamic
problem.

00:05:59.790 --> 00:06:04.510
One method to reduce instabilities and to
prevent pressure waves from amplifying off

00:06:04.510 --> 00:06:09.440
the side walls, like a sound wave, was to
install baffles that separated the combustion

00:06:09.440 --> 00:06:11.099
chamber radially.

00:06:11.099 --> 00:06:16.130
Several iterations of baffles were designed,
but with no computational method available

00:06:16.130 --> 00:06:20.730
the engineers of the F1 engine turned to the
next best practical method.

00:06:20.730 --> 00:06:26.340
They set off tiny bombs inside the combustion
chamber during operation to quickly induce

00:06:26.340 --> 00:06:28.900
small combustion instabilities.

00:06:28.900 --> 00:06:33.810
The combustion chamber bomb was attached to
the injector plate with the detonator inside

00:06:33.810 --> 00:06:35.229
the combustion zone.

00:06:35.229 --> 00:06:40.590
The detonator was encased in nylon that would
ablate away in the heat of combustion over

00:06:40.590 --> 00:06:45.130
time, allowing the engine to reach full operation
before detonating.

00:06:45.130 --> 00:06:49.759
Resulting in a pressure wave that traveled
through the engine just as a combustion instability

00:06:49.759 --> 00:06:50.759
would.

00:06:50.759 --> 00:06:55.590
The engineers could test the effectiveness
of new baffle designs by timing how long it

00:06:55.590 --> 00:07:00.270
took for normal operation to resume after
the detonation.

00:07:00.270 --> 00:07:05.440
These baffles effectively acted as acoustic
dampeners, like the foam wedges we place in

00:07:05.440 --> 00:07:07.479
sound treated rooms.

00:07:07.479 --> 00:07:12.930
For decades engineers have been working to
prevent these uncontrollable dynamic detonations.

00:07:12.930 --> 00:07:16.160
But engineers and scientists are stubborn
kinds of people.

00:07:16.160 --> 00:07:20.710
When they hear “uncontrollable” all they
hear is “not yet controlled”.

00:07:20.710 --> 00:07:25.990
And rotating detonation engines effectively
use the exact phenomenon that these baffles

00:07:25.990 --> 00:07:30.840
were trying to prevent, allowing a traveling
shockwave to travel tangentially around a

00:07:30.840 --> 00:07:33.300
ring shaped combustion chamber.

00:07:33.300 --> 00:07:38.560
The appeal of using detonations as a combustion
and propulsion mechanism is precisely what

00:07:38.560 --> 00:07:41.960
makes them dangerous, their quick release
of energy.

00:07:41.960 --> 00:07:46.880
We can visualize the amount of energy we can
extract by comparing the combustion cycle

00:07:46.880 --> 00:07:52.780
of a hypothetical detonation engine and a
normal gas turbine engine on a pressure-volume

00:07:52.780 --> 00:07:53.780
chart.

00:07:53.780 --> 00:07:59.229
This chart simply plots the pressure and volume
of air as it travels through an engine.

00:07:59.229 --> 00:08:03.740
In a gas turbine, the air enters the engine
at this pressure and volume.

00:08:03.740 --> 00:08:07.130
A compressor decreases its volume at a constant
pressure.

00:08:07.130 --> 00:08:11.440
It then moves to the combustor where heat
is added in at a constant pressure.

00:08:11.440 --> 00:08:17.060
Then the gas passes through a turbine as it
expands until it reaches its initial condition.

00:08:17.060 --> 00:08:23.120
The energy we extracted can be found by finding
the area enclosed by these curves.

00:08:23.120 --> 00:08:28.599
In a detonation cycle we skip the combustor
stage completely, and instead the detonation

00:08:28.599 --> 00:08:32.519
wave increases the pressure of the gas almost
instantaneously.

00:08:32.519 --> 00:08:34.320
Spiking it much higher.

00:08:34.320 --> 00:08:37.940
This creates a much larger area inside the
curves.

00:08:37.940 --> 00:08:43.140
The theoretical differences in thermodynamic
efficiencies using this analysis method are

00:08:43.140 --> 00:08:44.760
immense.

00:08:44.760 --> 00:08:51.980
Using hydrogen as our gas, a gas turbine engine
has a thermodynamic efficiency of 36.9% while

00:08:51.980 --> 00:08:57.230
a detonation engine has a thermodynamic efficiency
of 59.3%.[5].

00:08:57.230 --> 00:09:02.500
Simply, this means that more energy is extracted
from the same amount of fuel.

00:09:02.500 --> 00:09:07.880
However, an increase in energy yield is of
no use to us if we can’t control it, or

00:09:07.880 --> 00:09:09.940
it simply destroys our engine.

00:09:09.940 --> 00:09:15.450
To harness his energy we needed to develop
new materials and manufacturing techniques.

00:09:15.450 --> 00:09:20.360
This is where a new material, specially developed
for metallic 3D printing comes into play.

00:09:20.360 --> 00:09:22.510
Data sheet on table GRCOP-42.

00:09:22.510 --> 00:09:26.980
A nasa developed alloy made up of niobium,
chromium and copper.

00:09:26.980 --> 00:09:32.589
A high strength metal alloy specially formulated
to not only have a very high melting point,

00:09:32.589 --> 00:09:35.140
but a high thermal conductivity.

00:09:35.140 --> 00:09:40.050
Meaning it takes more energy to melt it and
it conducts the energy away more efficiently

00:09:40.050 --> 00:09:45.260
to prevent itself from melting, and critically,
NASA has more recently developed a method

00:09:45.260 --> 00:09:51.720
of using it metallic 3D printing, meaning
we can create incredibly complex geometries

00:09:51.720 --> 00:09:54.240
with this new age material.

00:09:54.240 --> 00:09:56.800
Maintaining this reaction is incredibly difficult.

00:09:56.800 --> 00:10:02.290
The shockwave, by nature, is traveling incredibly
fast, and causes rapid changes of temperature

00:10:02.290 --> 00:10:03.610
and pressure.

00:10:03.610 --> 00:10:04.610
Take the injectors.

00:10:04.610 --> 00:10:09.920
They have to be designed to handle not only
high flow rates but also drastic changes in

00:10:09.920 --> 00:10:11.529
pressure and temperature.

00:10:11.529 --> 00:10:18.800
As the shockwave travels by condition change
from 0.01 Megapascals of pressure at 300 kelvin,

00:10:18.800 --> 00:10:22.149
to 2 megapascals of pressure and 3000 kelvin.

00:10:22.149 --> 00:10:26.300
That cycle happens one thousand times per
second.[7]

00:10:26.300 --> 00:10:30.920
Our copper based alloy can help us dissipate
that energy, but these inlets need to switch

00:10:30.920 --> 00:10:36.010
from injecting gas to resisting the pressure
of the shockwave in milliseconds.

00:10:36.010 --> 00:10:41.630
To manufacture an inlet capable of handling
these conditions the engineers needed 3D printing.

00:10:41.630 --> 00:10:47.350
A typically machined injector may look something
like this, but the designers of the rotating

00:10:47.350 --> 00:10:53.090
detonation engine need to handle back flow,
and for this they can turn to fluid diodes,

00:10:53.090 --> 00:10:57.600
which would be impossible to manufacture with
traditional cutting tools.

00:10:57.600 --> 00:11:03.050
The shape of this passage allows fuel to flow
easily in one direction, but when flow attempts

00:11:03.050 --> 00:11:08.360
to travel in the opposite direction it impinges
itself, resisting flow.

00:11:08.360 --> 00:11:11.250
Effectively it’s a valve with no moving
parts.

00:11:11.250 --> 00:11:13.870
A perfect solution for this application.

00:11:13.870 --> 00:11:14.870
[8]

00:11:14.870 --> 00:11:18.070
The use of metal 3d printing doesn't stop
at inlet design.

00:11:18.070 --> 00:11:23.570
Every part of the rotating detonation engine
requires delicate shaping to harness the power

00:11:23.570 --> 00:11:24.899
of detonation.

00:11:24.899 --> 00:11:29.880
From the combustion chamber design to ensure
the shockwave remains in rotating to the tiny

00:11:29.880 --> 00:11:32.530
cooling channels running through its walls.

00:11:32.530 --> 00:11:37.930
The rotating detonation engine that NASA developed
used a 3D-printed thrust chamber that was

00:11:37.930 --> 00:11:41.130
actively cooled using Deionized water.[3]

00:11:41.130 --> 00:11:44.010
It also features an aerospike nozzle.

00:11:44.010 --> 00:11:47.070
Aerospike nozzles are kinda like inside-out
nozzles.

00:11:47.070 --> 00:11:52.570
Instead of the traditional bell shaped nozzle,
it features a cone at the center of the exhaust.

00:11:52.570 --> 00:11:57.040
Aerospike nozzles allow the rocket to operate
more efficiently at a range of altitudes.

00:11:57.040 --> 00:12:01.540
To understand why, we need to cover some basics
of nozzle design.

00:12:01.540 --> 00:12:06.230
The bell nozzle operates optimally at a certain
external atmospheric pressure.

00:12:06.230 --> 00:12:11.260
To work at its most efficient we want the
exhaust pressure at the nozzle exit to equal

00:12:11.260 --> 00:12:13.529
the external atmospheric pressure.

00:12:13.529 --> 00:12:18.110
If the external pressure is higher than the
exhaust exit pressure we are operating in

00:12:18.110 --> 00:12:20.570
the overexpanded condition.

00:12:20.570 --> 00:12:24.589
Flow separation from the nozzle occurs and
energy is lost.

00:12:24.589 --> 00:12:28.810
If the external pressure is lower than the
exhaust exit pressure we are operating in

00:12:28.810 --> 00:12:33.990
the underexpanded condition, the gas expands
outwards outside of the nozzle and energy

00:12:33.990 --> 00:12:35.170
is lost.

00:12:35.170 --> 00:12:39.880
However if we match the exit pressure and
atmospheric pressure neither happens and exhaust

00:12:39.880 --> 00:12:43.900
velocity is maximized, making our engine more
efficient.

00:12:43.900 --> 00:12:48.180
We could fit rockets with variable geometry
nozzles like fighter jets, but this would

00:12:48.180 --> 00:12:51.540
add a lot of weight, complexity and cost.

00:12:51.540 --> 00:12:57.020
These nozzles are a lot bigger than fighter
jets and until very recently were single use.

00:12:57.020 --> 00:12:58.020
[9]

00:12:58.020 --> 00:13:02.490
Aerospikes provide a lighter simpler alternative
to variable geometry exhausts.

00:13:02.490 --> 00:13:06.860
Here the shape of the exhaust is dictated
by the external atmospheric pressure.

00:13:06.860 --> 00:13:11.840
At higher pressures, at lower altitudes, the
exhaust is compressed against the aerospike,

00:13:11.840 --> 00:13:14.760
which redirects the exhaust to point straight
down.

00:13:14.760 --> 00:13:19.930
No flow separation can occur as the pressure
is pushing it against the aerospike.

00:13:19.930 --> 00:13:24.720
As pressure lowers this expansion continues,
and the aerospike automatically compensates

00:13:24.720 --> 00:13:26.880
to external pressure changes.

00:13:26.880 --> 00:13:31.450
The exact science of these aerospike nozzles
is a lot more complicated than this and deserve

00:13:31.450 --> 00:13:34.880
a video entirely dedicated to them alone.

00:13:34.880 --> 00:13:41.980
But they are uniquely suited to rotating detonation
engines, as an aerospike engine, with this

00:13:41.980 --> 00:13:47.740
cone design, requires a circular combustion
chamber, which the rotating detonation engine

00:13:47.740 --> 00:13:49.220
also requires.

00:13:49.220 --> 00:13:54.250
However these is a more important reason,
to optimize the design of a bell nozzle we

00:13:54.250 --> 00:14:00.070
need a consistent chamber pressure in order
to optimize our expansion ratio, with the

00:14:00.070 --> 00:14:05.399
rotating detonation engine the chamber pressure
is constantly changing along the circumference

00:14:05.399 --> 00:14:07.280
of the combustion chamber.

00:14:07.280 --> 00:14:12.570
The aerospike's ability to adapt to external
atmospheric pressures also helps it adapt

00:14:12.570 --> 00:14:15.220
to internal chamber pressure changes.

00:14:15.220 --> 00:14:16.220
[10]

00:14:16.220 --> 00:14:19.680
There is a lot more to do to make rotating
detonation engines viable.

00:14:19.680 --> 00:14:24.170
These prototypes, while promising, struggle
to run for long enough to produce prolonged

00:14:24.170 --> 00:14:25.170
thrust.

00:14:25.170 --> 00:14:31.689
In 2021, Japan tested a rotating detonation
engine in space, which operated for 6 seconds,

00:14:31.689 --> 00:14:36.861
producing 500 Newtons of thrust, and while
this was a proof of concept it’s a long

00:14:36.861 --> 00:14:41.410
way from the millions of Newtons traditional
rocket engines can achieve.[11]

00:14:41.410 --> 00:14:44.900
The mechanics of detonations are still not
understood fully.

00:14:44.900 --> 00:14:49.840
This means that maintaining a stable detonation
front is extremely difficult.

00:14:49.840 --> 00:14:52.880
The system is inherently chaotic.

00:14:52.880 --> 00:14:57.500
Engineers are fighting against entropy to
control these powerful turbulent flow regimes.

00:14:57.500 --> 00:15:01.870
With each revolution of the shockwave more
chaos is introduced to the system.

00:15:01.870 --> 00:15:07.180
A single vortex on revolution one can cause
a ripple effect until the rotation becomes

00:15:07.180 --> 00:15:10.630
completely unstable by rotation 20.

00:15:10.630 --> 00:15:16.610
NASA even stated that their tests sometimes
fluctuate from have 2 to 5 co-rotating detonation

00:15:16.610 --> 00:15:19.529
waves in the combustion chamber at once.[12]

00:15:19.529 --> 00:15:23.529
This NASA test was a huge milestone for the
technology.

00:15:23.529 --> 00:15:28.029
They demonstrated that their copper based
3D printed material could survive for long

00:15:28.029 --> 00:15:33.720
enough in this environment with their longest
run lasting nearly 2 minutes, and they managed

00:15:33.720 --> 00:15:40.670
to successfully initiate the detonation front
multiple times with 17 restarts over 600 seconds.

00:15:40.670 --> 00:15:45.300
The biggest challenge facing engineers of
rotating detonation engines is creating flow

00:15:45.300 --> 00:15:50.319
models to predict these instabilities and
developing methods to dampen and control these

00:15:50.319 --> 00:15:55.400
combustion instabilities, just as the engineers
of the Apollo program did all the way back

00:15:55.400 --> 00:15:57.120
in the 1960s.

00:15:57.120 --> 00:16:00.100
The next real engineering video is ready right
now.

